module gunbc.fleet.runtime_identity_match

// WHO IS ON THE NETWORK (cut 6, S10 dry half, of gunbc.plans.physical_machine_boot_program).
//
// A unit has been network-booted. Something now has to bind "the machine that answered on the network" to
// "the unit the attempt was made for", because every later step -- enrolling it, installing on it, letting
// the control plane act through it -- acts on the ANSWER of that binding. This module is the binding's
// EVIDENCE HALF: the axes observable before the boot from the firmware side, the same axes reported by the
// running OS afterwards, and a total match fn whose three verdicts mean three different things.
//
// WHY THE THREE VERDICTS ARE NOT TWO, which is the whole cut:
// Unprovable is not Mismatch. A unit whose BMC never answered the serial query, or whose OS image does not
// ship the tool that reads a model string, has told us NOTHING -- it has not told us it is a different
// machine. Reporting that as a mismatch condemns a unit for its BMC's silence, and in this fleet BMC
// silence is routine (S2's silent-BMC defect, and the rows where a controller rejects a query outright).
// So: a divergence between two readings is Mismatch; a missing reading on either side is Unprovable; only
// agreement across everything that had to be checked is Match. The refusal is preserved as itself rather
// than collapsed into absence, because "the controller said no" and "nobody asked" lead to different human
// actions.
//
// WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT:
// - NO IDENTITY DATA HERE. No row in this module carries a serial, a model, or an address: fixtures live in
//   witness fns only. A module that shipped a unit's real identity would be a second, unauditable register
//   of who the fleet's machines are, next to the intake subject that already owns that.
// - NO PER-HOST BRANCHING. Nothing here is keyed on a machine name, and no axis policy varies by unit. The
//   unit is CARRIED (as data, on both sides) and never matched on: the proof is entirely in the axes.
// - NO TRANSPORT IN THE DECISION. Nothing that decides a verdict is a transport fact: no command line, no
//   tool, no protocol verb appears in any type or fn here. The post-boot side does carry WHICH KIND OF
//   CHANNEL reported -- as a semantic axis, in one place -- because a channel determines which axes are
//   answerable at all, and an axis is the only shape where a report and its channel cannot contradict each
//   other. Deciding is done on the readings; the channel is context that is stated, never inferred.

import std.types { Bool, List, NonEmptyStr, String }
import std.optional { Optional, Present, Absent }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, decl_ref }
import extdeps.network.mac { Eui48Address, render_mac_address }

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE AXES
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The identity axes a boot attempt can be proven on. Closed, because the verdict must be accountable to
// a fixed list: a verdict that could omit an axis silently would be a verdict about whatever happened to be
// asked. An additional axis (a TPM attestation quote, say) adds an arm here, a union below, a field on
// each side, and a row in the axis table -- and the match fn's totals stay total because the comparison is
// a fold over this union rather than a hand-written chain.
type RuntimeIdentityAxis
  = BoardSerialAxis
  | BiosVersionAxis
  | ModelAxis
  | NicSetAxis

// How much an axis carries, as a policy the table can state rather than a bool the fold guesses with.
//
// DECISIVE axes must be read on BOTH sides and must agree, or there is no binding. CORROBORATING axes are
// checked only for divergence: a corroborating axis that goes unanswered does not void the binding, and a
// corroborating axis that CONTRADICTS does. That asymmetry is the point -- treating a corroborating gap as
// fatal would make every unit with a mute BMC unprovable forever, and treating a corroborating divergence
// as harmless would let a wrong machine through on the strength of a serial.
type AxisWeight
  = DecisiveAxis
  | CorroboratingAxis

// How two readings of one axis are compared, as policy rather than as code scattered through the fold.
//
// The MAC set is NOT an exact-equality axis: a firmware reports every interface it can boot from while the
// booted OS reports the interfaces it brought up, and on the fleet's boxes those sets legitimately differ
// (a management NIC the firmware lists and the OS leaves down). Requiring exact equality there would be
// Unprovable for every real unit; requiring NOTHING there would let a different machine through. So the
// rule is a shared address, and it is stated in the table where a reviewer can read it, not buried in the
// comparison.
type AxisComparison
  = ExactStringEquality
  | NonEmptyMacIntersection

// ONE ROW PER AXIS: what it weighs, how it is compared, and who says so.
type RuntimeIdentityAxisRow {
  axis: RuntimeIdentityAxis
  weight: AxisWeight
  comparison: AxisComparison
  authority: DeclarationRef
}

// THE AXIS TABLE, as authored evidence. Every row cites a declaration that EXISTS and RESOLVES: the
// serial row cites the intake subject's own serial observation (the authority that already says board
// serial is what survives a DIMM change), the firmware-version row cites the intake module that reads a
// BMC firmware version, the model row cites the intake module that treats a hardware label as bound to
// an electronic identity rather than as one, and the address row cites the client identity the delivery
// module keys on an address. A row that cited nothing would be a policy nobody can audit; a row that
// cited a name nobody declares would be worse, because it would LOOK audited (DESIGN section 3b: a home
// that does not resolve is a citation defect). A future requirement that a fourth axis become decisive
// adds a row with a different weight -- no fn in this module changes, which is what makes the policy
// reviewable without reading the fold.
data runtime_identity_axis_rows: List<RuntimeIdentityAxisRow> = [
  RuntimeIdentityAxisRow {
    axis: BoardSerialAxis,
    weight: DecisiveAxis,
    comparison: ExactStringEquality,
    authority: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.subject", decl_name: "BoardSerialObservation"),
  },
  RuntimeIdentityAxisRow {
    axis: BiosVersionAxis,
    weight: CorroboratingAxis,
    comparison: ExactStringEquality,
    authority: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.ampere_dram_console_observation", decl_name: "AmpereDramBlock"),
  },
  RuntimeIdentityAxisRow {
    axis: ModelAxis,
    weight: CorroboratingAxis,
    comparison: ExactStringEquality,
    authority: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.subject", decl_name: "PhysicalDimmLabelBinding"),
  },
  RuntimeIdentityAxisRow {
    axis: NicSetAxis,
    weight: DecisiveAxis,
    comparison: NonEmptyMacIntersection,
    authority: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.network_boot_delivery", decl_name: "ObservedDhcpPxeClient"),
  },
]

fn runtime_identity_axis_row(axis: RuntimeIdentityAxis) -> RuntimeIdentityAxisRow? {
  runtime_identity_axis_rows.filter(r => same_runtime_identity_axis(left: r.axis, right: axis)).first()
}

// Structural equality over a closed union, written as a match rather than delegated: an arm that forgot a
// case is a compile error, and the fold's per-axis lookups depend on this being exact.
fn same_runtime_identity_axis(left: RuntimeIdentityAxis, right: RuntimeIdentityAxis) -> Bool {
  match left {
    BoardSerialAxis => match right { BoardSerialAxis => true _ => false }
    BiosVersionAxis => match right { BiosVersionAxis => true _ => false }
    ModelAxis => match right { ModelAxis => true _ => false }
    NicSetAxis => match right { NicSetAxis => true _ => false }
  }
}

data runtime_identity_match_law: String = "Cut 6 S10 ruling: Mismatch requires two readings that disagree; a missing reading on either side is Unprovable, never Mismatch, because a unit must not be condemned for its BMC's silence. Corroborating axes are checked for divergence only; decisive axes must be read on both sides and agree. Unit identity is carried as data and never matched on; no row of this module names a machine."

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// EACH AXIS, AS A STANDING -- one union each, with the three states as ARMS
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// What a controller's or an OS's "no" is on an identity axis. Closed, because the remedy differs: a query
// the controller does not implement is a row to pin, an authentication failure is an operator action, and
// an unreadable reply is a defect in the reader. A string cannot split those.
type RuntimeIdentityRefusal
  = QueryUnsupported { detail: NonEmptyStr }
  | AuthenticationFailed { detail: NonEmptyStr }
  | ReplyUnreadable { detail: NonEmptyStr }

fn runtime_identity_refusal_detail(refusal: RuntimeIdentityRefusal) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match refusal {
    QueryUnsupported { detail: d } => d
    AuthenticationFailed { detail: d } => d
    ReplyUnreadable { detail: d } => d
  }
}

// THE BOARD-SERIAL AXIS. The serial as one side reports it, verbatim; normalization would be a second
// authority on what the string says, so equality is exact and the reading carries the raw value.
type RuntimeBoardSerialStanding
  = BoardSerialObserved { serial: NonEmptyStr }
  | BoardSerialUnobserved { reason: NonEmptyStr }
  | BoardSerialRefused { refusal: RuntimeIdentityRefusal }

// THE FIRMWARE-VERSION AXIS. Same vocabulary the readiness seam uses for the same fact: the version the
// unit reports for itself, as a reading rather than as a claim about a release.
type RuntimeBiosVersionStanding
  = BiosVersionRead { version: NonEmptyStr }
  | BiosVersionMissing { reason: NonEmptyStr }
  | BiosVersionQueryRefused { refusal: RuntimeIdentityRefusal }

// THE MODEL AXIS: the board/product string one side reports. Corroborating by table, because a firmware
// can report a generic string for several boards while a serial cannot.
type RuntimeModelStanding
  = ModelRead { model: NonEmptyStr }
  | ModelMissing { reason: NonEmptyStr }
  | ModelQueryRefused { refusal: RuntimeIdentityRefusal }

// THE ADDRESS SET AXIS. Observed carries the addresses that side reported; an empty list is a side that
// reported no addresses at all, which is a reading, not a gap -- and the table's intersection rule turns it
// into a divergence rather than a shrug.
type RuntimeNicSetStanding
  = NicSetReported { addresses: List<Eui48Address> }
  | NicSetMissing { reason: NonEmptyStr }
  | NicSetQueryRefused { refusal: RuntimeIdentityRefusal }

// What an axis did not answer, and WHETHER it stayed quiet or was refused. The absence is a SUM, not a
// string: `Unread` (nobody got an answer) and `ControllerRefused` (an answer of "no" came back, with its
// reason) lead to different human actions, and once both are flattened into one `NonEmptyStr` the closed
// refusal sum cannot be recovered from the gap -- a declared variant that is then erased is the anemic
// modeling DESIGN section 2 forbids. The witness
// `test_refusal_is_unprovable_and_preserves_its_kind` is what keeps this honest.
type RuntimeIdentityAbsence
  = Unread { reason: NonEmptyStr }
  | ControllerRefused { refusal: RuntimeIdentityRefusal }

// Which axis did not answer, on which side, and whether it was silence or a refusal.
type RuntimeIdentityGap {
  axis: RuntimeIdentityAxis
  side: IdentitySide
  absence: RuntimeIdentityAbsence
}

// WHICH SIDE of the boot a reading comes from. Named, so an Unprovable can say whether the firmware side
// or the running-OS side is the one that stayed quiet -- the operator's next action differs completely.
type IdentitySide
  = PreBootFirmwareSide
  | PostBootOsSide

// A divergence, with BOTH readings. The pair is what makes a Mismatch actionable: "the serials differ" is
// an accusation, "the BMC says X and the OS says Y" is a diagnosis.
type RuntimeIdentityDivergence {
  axis: RuntimeIdentityAxis
  pre_boot: NonEmptyStr
  post_boot: NonEmptyStr
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE TWO SIDES
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// THE PRE-BOOT STANDING: what the firmware side said before the boot, per axis. Every axis is present as a
// field, and each field is itself a three-way union, so there is no way to state a pre-boot standing that
// quietly omits an axis -- and no way to claim a value while admitting nothing was read.
//
// The unit is CARRIED here and on the other side, and the match fn never compares the two keys: this is a
// proof about the machine's own reportings, not a check that two labels were spelled the same.
type PreBootIdentityStanding {
  unit: NonEmptyStr
  board_serial: RuntimeBoardSerialStanding
  bios_version: RuntimeBiosVersionStanding
  model: RuntimeModelStanding
  nics: RuntimeNicSetStanding
}

// THE POST-BOOT REPORT: the same four axes, as the running OS reports them.
type PostBootIdentityReport {
  unit: NonEmptyStr
  reported_over: PostBootReportChannel
  board_serial: RuntimeBoardSerialStanding
  bios_version: RuntimeBiosVersionStanding
  model: RuntimeModelStanding
  nics: RuntimeNicSetStanding
}

// HOW the OS came to report. A semantic axis rather than a transport detail, because it changes what the
// report can cover: a machine reachable only over a serial console has no session that can read every axis,
// and a gap on such a unit is expected rather than suspicious. It stays an axis so the reading and the
// channel cannot disagree about themselves -- a report cannot claim a full axis set over a channel that
// cannot carry one, because there is exactly one place both are stated.
type PostBootReportChannel
  = NetworkSessionChannel
  | SerialConsoleChannel

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE VERDICT
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Three verdicts, closed, each carrying what makes it true.
//
// Match carries the axes it actually checked and passed -- not a bare "true", so a Match can be audited
// against the table afterwards. Mismatch carries both readings for every axis that diverged. Unprovable
// carries the named gaps that prevented a verdict, which is what makes "we do not know" a reportable state
// rather than a silent false.
type RuntimeIdentityVerdict
  = Match { axes_matched: List<RuntimeIdentityAxis>, unit: NonEmptyStr }
  | Mismatch { diverged: List<RuntimeIdentityDivergence>, unit: NonEmptyStr }
  | Unprovable { why: List<RuntimeIdentityGap>, unit: NonEmptyStr }

// THE MATCH FN. Total: no error type in its signature, nothing that can panic in its body (no indexing, no
// unwrapping, every match exhaustive over closed unions), and it takes ONLY the two sides -- the axis table
// is read from the catalog this module owns, so a caller cannot weaken the verdict by passing a friendlier
// policy.
//
// The order of the three checks IS the ruling:
//  1. Divergences first. Two readings that disagree is the strongest evidence available about the pair, and
//     it outranks any gap: a unit with a wrong serial is a Mismatch even if its model string never came
//     back. Silence never launders a contradiction.
//  2. Gaps next, and only over axes the table makes DECISIVE -- plus any corroborating gap is recorded but
//     cannot be fatal, so an unanswered corroborating axis leaves the verdict Match rather than dragging an
//     honest unit into Unprovable.
//  3. Match last, and only with the checked-and-passed axes listed.
fn runtime_identity_match(
  pre: PreBootIdentityStanding,
  post: PostBootIdentityReport,
) -> RuntimeIdentityVerdict {
  let diverged = all_divergences(pre: pre, post: post)
  let gaps = fatal_gaps(pre: pre, post: post)
  if diverged.length() > 0 {
    Mismatch { diverged: diverged, unit: pre.unit }
  } else if gaps.length() > 0 {
    Unprovable { why: gaps, unit: pre.unit }
  } else {
    Match { axes_matched: axes_checked_and_passed(pre: pre, post: post), unit: pre.unit }
  }
}

// Every axis where BOTH sides read and the readings disagree, in table order. A flat_map over the table
// rather than a filter/map pair over a probe union: an axis with nothing to report contributes NO element,
// so there is no "unreachable" placeholder value anywhere in this module that a bug could surface as a
// verdict.
fn all_divergences(pre: PreBootIdentityStanding, post: PostBootIdentityReport) -> List<RuntimeIdentityDivergence> {
  runtime_identity_axis_rows.flat_map(r => divergence_of_row(row: r, pre: pre, post: post))
}

// The gaps that can void a verdict: a gap on either side of any DECISIVE axis. Corroborating gaps are
// deliberately not gathered here -- that is where "a mute BMC does not condemn a unit" lives.
fn fatal_gaps(pre: PreBootIdentityStanding, post: PostBootIdentityReport) -> List<RuntimeIdentityGap> {
  runtime_identity_axis_rows.flat_map(r => fatal_gap_of_row(row: r, pre: pre, post: post))
}

// The axes a Match may claim: decisive axes, read on both sides, compared and passed. A corroborating axis
// that agreed is not claimed, because it was not required -- claiming it would let a Match look stronger
// than the table says it is.
fn axes_checked_and_passed(pre: PreBootIdentityStanding, post: PostBootIdentityReport) -> List<RuntimeIdentityAxis> {
  runtime_identity_axis_rows.filter(r => axis_is_decisive(row: r)).filter(r => both_sides_read(row: r, pre: pre, post: post)).map(r => r.axis)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PER-AXIS READINGS AND COMPARISONS
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// An axis contributes AT MOST ONE divergence and AT MOST ONE fatal gap, expressed as an Optional that the
// fold flat_maps. There is deliberately no "no divergence" value carrying a dummy divergence, and no
// unreachable placeholder string anywhere in this module: an axis with nothing to report contributes no
// element, so a verdict can never be assembled from a fabricated reading.
fn divergence_of_row(row: RuntimeIdentityAxisRow, pre: PreBootIdentityStanding, post: PostBootIdentityReport) -> List<RuntimeIdentityDivergence> {
  match axis_pair(row: row, pre: pre, post: post) {
    Present { value: pair } => if axes_agree(row: row, pair: pair) {
      []
    } else {
      match pair {
        TextPair { pre_text: pt, post_text: po } => [RuntimeIdentityDivergence { axis: row.axis, pre_boot: pt, post_boot: po }]
        AddressSetPair { pre_addresses: pa, post_addresses: po } => [RuntimeIdentityDivergence { axis: row.axis, pre_boot: render_mac_set(addresses: pa), post_boot: render_mac_set(addresses: po) }]
      }
    }
    Absent => []
  }
}

fn fatal_gap_of_row(row: RuntimeIdentityAxisRow, pre: PreBootIdentityStanding, post: PostBootIdentityReport) -> List<RuntimeIdentityGap> {
  if axis_is_decisive(row: row) {
    match side_gap(row: row, pre: pre, post: post) {
      Present { value: g } => [g]
      Absent => []
    }
  } else {
    []
  }
}

fn axis_is_decisive(row: RuntimeIdentityAxisRow) -> Bool {
  match row.weight {
    DecisiveAxis => true
    CorroboratingAxis => false
  }
}

fn both_sides_read(row: RuntimeIdentityAxisRow, pre: PreBootIdentityStanding, post: PostBootIdentityReport) -> Bool {
  match axis_pair(row: row, pre: pre, post: post) {
    Present { value: _ } => true
    Absent => false
  }
}

// THE READINGS OF ONE AXIS ON BOTH SIDES, or Absent when either side failed to read.
//
// A SUM, not a record with four fields two of which are always placeholders. The two comparison policies
// consume different evidence -- an exact-string axis has no addresses to compare, an address-set axis has
// no text to compare -- and one flat record would force every string axis to carry `[]` where addresses
// should be and every address axis to carry a rendering where a reading should be. A flat shape then lets
// a comparison read the half its policy does not use: the divergence this shape makes impossible is a
// MAC axis "agreeing" because two rendered strings happened to match, or a serial axis comparing empty
// address lists and calling that a shared interface. Which arm a pair is follows from the axis's policy in
// the table, and `axes_agree` cannot ask for evidence its arm does not carry.
type AxisReadingPair
  = TextPair { pre_text: NonEmptyStr, post_text: NonEmptyStr }
  | AddressSetPair { pre_addresses: List<Eui48Address>, post_addresses: List<Eui48Address> }

// THE ONLY PLACE A READING IS EXTRACTED from either side, per axis. One place, so the fold cannot compare
// one side's field against the other side's different field -- the bug this shape exists to prevent.
//
// The MAC axis decides on addresses and REPORTS a rendering: `render_mac_address` never enters a
// comparison, only a divergence's two texts. So the divergence an address-set axis produces carries the
// two sets themselves (what was actually compared) plus the renderings (what an operator reads).
fn axis_pair(
  row: RuntimeIdentityAxisRow,
  pre: PreBootIdentityStanding,
  post: PostBootIdentityReport,
) -> AxisReadingPair? {
  match row.axis {
    BoardSerialAxis => match pre.board_serial {
      BoardSerialObserved { serial: ps } => match post.board_serial {
        BoardSerialObserved { serial: os } => Present { value: TextPair { pre_text: ps, post_text: os } }
        _ => Absent
      }
      _ => Absent
    }
    BiosVersionAxis => match pre.bios_version {
      BiosVersionRead { version: pv } => match post.bios_version {
        BiosVersionRead { version: ov } => Present { value: TextPair { pre_text: pv, post_text: ov } }
        _ => Absent
      }
      _ => Absent
    }
    ModelAxis => match pre.model {
      ModelRead { model: pm } => match post.model {
        ModelRead { model: om } => Present { value: TextPair { pre_text: pm, post_text: om } }
        _ => Absent
      }
      _ => Absent
    }
    NicSetAxis => match pre.nics {
      NicSetReported { addresses: pa } => match post.nics {
        NicSetReported { addresses: oa } => Present { value: AddressSetPair { pre_addresses: pa, post_addresses: oa } }
        _ => Absent
      }
      _ => Absent
    }
  }
}

// The comparison, dispatched on the table's policy rather than on the axis: the table says how to compare,
// and this fn is the one implementation of each rule.
//
// WHICH ARM CAN ARRIVE is settled upstream by the same policy -- axis_pair builds the pair from the row --
// so the cross combinations below are unreachable. They FAIL CLOSED rather than being written as an
// assertion or a panic: an axis that somehow reached a rule with evidence of the other kind must not be
// blessed as agreeing, it must surface as a divergence an operator sees. Fail-closed is also the only
// choice that keeps the fn total, which the fold's totality depends on.
fn axes_agree(row: RuntimeIdentityAxisRow, pair: AxisReadingPair) -> Bool {
  match pair {
    TextPair { pre_text: pt, post_text: pt2 } => match row.comparison {
      ExactStringEquality => pt == pt2
      NonEmptyMacIntersection => false
    }
    AddressSetPair { pre_addresses: pa, post_addresses: po } => match row.comparison {
      NonEmptyMacIntersection => mac_sets_share_an_address(pre: pa, post: po)
      ExactStringEquality => false
    }
  }
}

fn mac_sets_share_an_address(pre: List<Eui48Address>, post: List<Eui48Address>) -> Bool {
  pre.any(a => post.any(b => same_mac_address(left: a, right: b)))
}

// Addresses compared octet by octet, so the comparison is about the address and not about how it was
// rendered -- render_mac_address is for reporting, never for deciding.
fn same_mac_address(left: Eui48Address, right: Eui48Address) -> Bool {
  left.octet1 == right.octet1
    && left.octet2 == right.octet2
    && left.octet3 == right.octet3
    && left.octet4 == right.octet4
    && left.octet5 == right.octet5
    && left.octet6 == right.octet6
}

// The rendering of an address set, for REPORTING only -- never for deciding: a divergence is reported in
// the same spelling an operator sees on the wire, but the comparison above reads addresses.
// `render_mac_address` is the single authority for how one address is written.
fn render_mac_set(addresses: List<Eui48Address>) -> NonEmptyStr {
  join(addresses.map(a => render_mac_address(addr: a)), ",") as NonEmptyStr
}

// Which side stayed quiet, and what it said. Both sides are checked so an Unprovable is located rather than
// merely asserted; the pre-boot side is asked first because in this fleet the firmware is the side that
// usually refuses.
fn side_gap(
  row: RuntimeIdentityAxisRow,
  pre: PreBootIdentityStanding,
  post: PostBootIdentityReport,
) -> RuntimeIdentityGap? {
  match pre_side_gap(row: row, pre: pre) {
    Present { value: g } => Present { value: g }
    Absent => post_side_gap(row: row, post: post)
  }
}

// Which side stayed quiet, and whether it was silence or a refusal. Both sides are checked so an Unprovable
// is located rather than merely asserted; the pre-boot side is asked first because in this fleet the
// firmware is the side that usually refuses.
// WHY AN AXIS DID NOT ANSWER, derived from the axis union itself rather than from a caller's string. Each
// of the four axis unions already distinguishes "not read" from "refused"; these fns carry that
// distinction through into the gap instead of collapsing both arms into text. One fn per union, so adding
// an axis adds an fn here and cannot silently drop a refusal arm -- the match is exhaustive.
fn board_serial_absence(standing: RuntimeBoardSerialStanding) -> RuntimeIdentityAbsence? {
  match standing {
    BoardSerialObserved { serial: _ } => Absent
    BoardSerialUnobserved { reason: r } => Present { value: Unread { reason: r } }
    BoardSerialRefused { refusal: f } => Present { value: ControllerRefused { refusal: f } }
  }
}

fn bios_version_absence(standing: RuntimeBiosVersionStanding) -> RuntimeIdentityAbsence? {
  match standing {
    BiosVersionRead { version: _ } => Absent
    BiosVersionMissing { reason: r } => Present { value: Unread { reason: r } }
    BiosVersionQueryRefused { refusal: f } => Present { value: ControllerRefused { refusal: f } }
  }
}

fn model_absence(standing: RuntimeModelStanding) -> RuntimeIdentityAbsence? {
  match standing {
    ModelRead { model: _ } => Absent
    ModelMissing { reason: r } => Present { value: Unread { reason: r } }
    ModelQueryRefused { refusal: f } => Present { value: ControllerRefused { refusal: f } }
  }
}

fn nic_set_absence(standing: RuntimeNicSetStanding) -> RuntimeIdentityAbsence? {
  match standing {
    NicSetReported { addresses: _ } => Absent
    NicSetMissing { reason: r } => Present { value: Unread { reason: r } }
    NicSetQueryRefused { refusal: f } => Present { value: ControllerRefused { refusal: f } }
  }
}

fn pre_side_gap(row: RuntimeIdentityAxisRow, pre: PreBootIdentityStanding) -> RuntimeIdentityGap? {
  match absence_on_firmware_side(row: row, pre: pre) {
    Present { value: a } => Present { value: RuntimeIdentityGap { axis: row.axis, side: PreBootFirmwareSide, absence: a } }
    Absent => Absent
  }
}

fn absence_on_firmware_side(row: RuntimeIdentityAxisRow, pre: PreBootIdentityStanding) -> RuntimeIdentityAbsence? {
  match row.axis {
    BoardSerialAxis => board_serial_absence(standing: pre.board_serial)
    BiosVersionAxis => bios_version_absence(standing: pre.bios_version)
    ModelAxis => model_absence(standing: pre.model)
    NicSetAxis => nic_set_absence(standing: pre.nics)
  }
}

fn post_side_gap(row: RuntimeIdentityAxisRow, post: PostBootIdentityReport) -> RuntimeIdentityGap? {
  match absence_on_os_side(row: row, post: post) {
    Present { value: a } => Present { value: RuntimeIdentityGap { axis: row.axis, side: PostBootOsSide, absence: a } }
    Absent => Absent
  }
}

fn absence_on_os_side(row: RuntimeIdentityAxisRow, post: PostBootIdentityReport) -> RuntimeIdentityAbsence? {
  match row.axis {
    BoardSerialAxis => board_serial_absence(standing: post.board_serial)
    BiosVersionAxis => bios_version_absence(standing: post.bios_version)
    ModelAxis => model_absence(standing: post.model)
    NicSetAxis => nic_set_absence(standing: post.nics)
  }
}


// HOW a gap failed, as data rather than as a string a consumer would have to re-parse. A caller that must
// route "the controller said no" to a row-pin and "nobody asked" to a probe fix reads these; both are
// derivable from the sum, and neither is stored beside it.
fn runtime_identity_gap_is_refusal(gap: RuntimeIdentityGap) -> Bool {
  match gap.absence {
    ControllerRefused { refusal: _ } => true
    Unread { reason: _ } => false
  }
}

fn runtime_identity_gap_refusal(gap: RuntimeIdentityGap) -> RuntimeIdentityRefusal? {
  match gap.absence {
    ControllerRefused { refusal: f } => Present { value: f }
    Unread { reason: _ } => Absent
  }
}

// The gap rendered for a human, derived from the sum on demand. Renderings are always derived from the
// semantic arms and never stored next to them.
fn runtime_identity_gap_detail(gap: RuntimeIdentityGap) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match gap.absence {
    Unread { reason: r } => r
    ControllerRefused { refusal: f } => runtime_identity_refusal_detail(refusal: f)
  }
}

// Whether a verdict is a Match, as an fn rather than as a field: a caller cannot ask a verdict whether it
// matched and get a stored answer that drifted from its arms.
fn runtime_identity_verdict_is_match(verdict: RuntimeIdentityVerdict) -> Bool {
  match verdict {
    Match { axes_matched: _, unit: _ } => true
    Mismatch { diverged: _, unit: _ } => false
    Unprovable { why: _, unit: _ } => false
  }
}

fn runtime_identity_verdict_is_unprovable(verdict: RuntimeIdentityVerdict) -> Bool {
  match verdict {
    Unprovable { why: _, unit: _ } => true
    Match { axes_matched: _, unit: _ } => false
    Mismatch { diverged: _, unit: _ } => false
  }
}

fn runtime_identity_verdict_is_mismatch(verdict: RuntimeIdentityVerdict) -> Bool {
  match verdict {
    Mismatch { diverged: _, unit: _ } => true
    Match { axes_matched: _, unit: _ } => false
    Unprovable { why: _, unit: _ } => false
  }
}

fn runtime_identity_verdict_unit(verdict: RuntimeIdentityVerdict) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match verdict {
    Match { axes_matched: _, unit: u } => u
    Mismatch { diverged: _, unit: u } => u
    Unprovable { why: _, unit: u } => u
  }
}
